  "preface": {
    "purpose": "Pandora's Box is a structured behavioral dataset for AI agent modeling of interpersonal dynamics. It defines distinguishable agent archetypes along three binary dimensional axes, providing for each type: a core identity matrix, behavioral state machine, communication protocols, physical escalation ladder, trigger-response database, decision-tree logic (Python), emotional trigger sequences, resistance patterns, and long-term maintenance protocols.\n\n**Intended use:** Behavioral simulation, classification of observed interaction patterns, and training data for conversational AI systems operating in social/dating contexts.\n\n**Scope:** This edition profiles three agent types (TDI, TJI, NDI) — all from the Idealist (I) branch of the Relational Worldview axis. The Realist (R) branch types (TDR, TJR, NDR, NJR) and the NJI type are not profiled in this edition.",
    "methodology": "Agent profiles were constructed from synthesized behavioral observation patterns, organized into a uniform 9-section template to enable cross-agent comparison. Weight values (0.0–1.0) represent relative salience/confidence for each trait within the type. Activation thresholds represent the modeled stimulus intensity required to trigger emotional state transitions.",
    "howToUse": "1. **Classify:** Use the Dimensional Axes (§1.1) and Type Code Legend (§0.5) to determine which agent type best matches an observed subject.\n\n2. **Predict:** Use the Behavioral State Machine (§x.2) to anticipate state transitions given stimuli.\n\n3. **Respond:** Use the Communication Protocol Matrix (§x.3) and Trigger-Response Database (§x.5) to select appropriate interaction strategies.\n\n4. **Distinguish:** Use Part III (Cross-Agent Analysis) when two types present similar surface behaviors.",
    "disclaimer": "This document models behavioral patterns for AI simulation purposes. It describes archetypal patterns, not prescriptions for real human interaction. All interpersonal engagement should be grounded in mutual respect, clear communication, and enthusiastic consent. The escalation frameworks herein describe modeled patterns, not recommended conduct.\n\n---"
  },
